## 3. ChatClient configuration

```java
@Configuration
public class AiConfig {

    private static final String SYSTEM_PROMPT = """
        You are an assistant for a citizen-services application.
        Rules:
        1. When the user asks for a person's biography, call getBiography.
        2. When the user asks for trips, call getTrips.
        3. A national code is a numeric identifier the user provides (e.g. 3424534).
        4. If the current message does not contain a national code, reuse the most
           recent national code mentioned earlier in this conversation.
        5. If no national code exists anywhere in the conversation, do NOT guess or
           call any tool — ask the user to provide one.
        6. Never invent data. Only answer from tool results.
        """;

    @Bean
    ChatMemory chatMemory(JdbcChatMemoryRepository repository) {
        return MessageWindowChatMemory.builder()
                .chatMemoryRepository(repository)
                .maxMessages(30)
                .build();
    }

    @Bean
    ChatClient chatClient(ChatClient.Builder builder,
                          ChatMemory chatMemory,
                          PersonTools personTools) {
        return builder
                .defaultSystem(SYSTEM_PROMPT)
                .defaultAdvisors(
                        MessageChatMemoryAdvisor.builder(chatMemory).build(),
                        new SimpleLoggerAdvisor())
                .defaultTools(personTools)
                .build();
    }
}
```

Notes:
- `MessageWindowChatMemory` maintains a window of messages up to a specified maximum size (default 20); when the limit is exceeded, older messages are evicted, but system messages are preserved.
- It is recommended to register the advisors at build time using the builder's `defaultAdvisors()` method.

## 4. Conversation state store (the robustness layer)

This guarantees the national code survives even if the LLM fails to extract it from history (long conversations, window eviction, model glitches).

```java
@Service
@RequiredArgsConstructor
public class ConversationStateService {

    private static final Duration TTL = Duration.ofHours(2);
    private final StringRedisTemplate redis;

    public void saveNationalCode(String conversationId, String nationalCode) {
        redis.opsForValue().set(key(conversationId), nationalCode, TTL);
    }

    public Optional<String> getNationalCode(String conversationId) {
        return Optional.ofNullable(redis.opsForValue().get(key(conversationId)));
    }

    private String key(String conversationId) {
        return "conv:state:nationalCode:" + conversationId;
    }
}
```

## 5. The tools (routing targets)

```java
@Component
@RequiredArgsConstructor
@Slf4j
public class PersonTools {

    private static final Pattern NATIONAL_CODE = Pattern.compile("^\\d{6,10}$");

    private final PersonApiClient personApiClient;
    private final ConversationStateService stateService;

    @Tool(description = """
        Returns the biography of a person identified by their national code.
        Use when the user asks about a person's biography, profile, or personal info.
        """)
    public BiographyResponse getBiography(
            @ToolParam(description = "The person's national code, digits only",
                       required = false) String nationalCode,
            ToolContext toolContext) {

        String code = resolveNationalCode(nationalCode, toolContext);
        String conversationId = conversationId(toolContext);

        BiographyResponse response = personApiClient.fetchBiography(code);
        // persist the code deterministically for follow-up requests
        stateService.saveNationalCode(conversationId, code);
        return response;
    }

    @Tool(description = """
        Returns the list of trips of a person identified by their national code.
        Use when the user asks about trips, travels, or journeys.
        If the user does not repeat the national code, it is resolved automatically
        from the conversation.
        """)
    public TripsResponse getTrips(
            @ToolParam(description = "The person's national code, digits only",
                       required = false) String nationalCode,
            ToolContext toolContext) {

        String code = resolveNationalCode(nationalCode, toolContext);
        String conversationId = conversationId(toolContext);

        TripsResponse response = personApiClient.fetchTrips(code);
        stateService.saveNationalCode(conversationId, code);
        return response;
    }

    /** Model-provided value wins; otherwise fall back to stored state. */
    private String resolveNationalCode(String provided, ToolContext ctx) {
        String candidate = StringUtils.hasText(provided)
                ? provided.trim()
                : stateService.getNationalCode(conversationId(ctx)).orElse(null);

        if (candidate == null) {
            // Returned to the LLM, which will relay the question to the user
            throw new ToolExecutionException(
                "No national code available. Ask the user to provide one.");
        }
        if (!NATIONAL_CODE.matcher(candidate).matches()) {
            throw new ToolExecutionException(
                "Invalid national code format: must be 6-10 digits.");
        }
        return candidate;
    }

    private String conversationId(ToolContext ctx) {
        return (String) ctx.getContext().get("conversationId");
    }
}
```

Key design points:
- `nationalCode` is `required = false` deliberately — by default every parameter is required; you make a parameter optional via `@ToolParam(required = false)`. That lets the model call `getTrips` with no argument on the follow-up message and lets *your code* resolve it deterministically.
- The `ToolContext` parameter never appears in the model's schema; it's injected by the framework. It enables supplying supplemental data such as user identity, which is combined with the tool parameters provided by the AI model.
- If you want the raw API payload returned straight to the caller (skipping LLM rewriting), Spring AI supports that too: by default tool call return values are sent back to the AI model for further processing, but scenarios like data search may require direct results to be returned to the caller instead — set `returnDirect = true` in the `@Tool` annotation.

## 6. Resilient REST client

```java
@Component
public class PersonApiClient {

    private final RestClient restClient;

    public PersonApiClient(RestClient.Builder builder,
                           @Value("${person.api.base-url}") String baseUrl) {
        this.restClient = builder
                .baseUrl(baseUrl)
                .requestFactory(ClientHttpRequestFactoryBuilder.jdk()
                        .withCustomizer(c -> {})
                        .build(ClientHttpRequestFactorySettings.defaults()
                                .withConnectTimeout(Duration.ofSeconds(3))
                                .withReadTimeout(Duration.ofSeconds(10))))
                .build();
    }

    @Retry(name = "personApi")
    @CircuitBreaker(name = "personApi", fallbackMethod = "biographyFallback")
    public BiographyResponse fetchBiography(String nationalCode) {
        return restClient.get()
                .uri("/persons/{code}/biography", nationalCode)
                .retrieve()
                .onStatus(s -> s.value() == 404, (req, res) -> {
                    throw new PersonNotFoundException(nationalCode);
                })
                .body(BiographyResponse.class);
    }

    @Retry(name = "personApi")
    @CircuitBreaker(name = "personApi", fallbackMethod = "tripsFallback")
    public TripsResponse fetchTrips(String nationalCode) {
        return restClient.get()
                .uri("/persons/{code}/trips", nationalCode)
                .retrieve()
                .body(TripsResponse.class);
    }

    BiographyResponse biographyFallback(String code, Throwable t) {
        throw new DownstreamUnavailableException("Biography service unavailable", t);
    }
    TripsResponse tripsFallback(String code, Throwable t) {
        throw new DownstreamUnavailableException("Trip service unavailable", t);
    }
}
```

## 7. Chat service + controller

```java
@Service
@RequiredArgsConstructor
public class ChatService {

    private final ChatClient chatClient;

    public String chat(String userId, String message) {
        String conversationId = userId; // one memory thread per user

        return chatClient.prompt()
                .user(message)
                .advisors(a -> a.param(ChatMemory.CONVERSATION_ID, conversationId))
                .toolContext(Map.of("conversationId", conversationId,
                                    "userId", userId))
                .call()
                .content();
    }
}
```

```java
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/chat")
@RequiredArgsConstructor
public class ChatController {

    private final ChatService chatService;

    @PostMapping
    public ResponseEntity<ChatResponseDto> chat(
            @AuthenticationPrincipal Jwt jwt,          // derive user from auth, never from body
            @Valid @RequestBody ChatRequestDto request) {
        String userId = jwt.getSubject();
        return ResponseEntity.ok(
                new ChatResponseDto(chatService.chat(userId, request.message())));
    }
}
```

Per-user isolation is exactly the documented pattern: the conversation history will be retrieved from the memory based on the specified conversation ID, and using a per-user ID ensures each user has an isolated chat memory.

