## How your scenario plays out

| Turn | Message | What happens |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | "give me the biography of a person with national code 3424534" | LLM routes to `getBiography(nationalCode="3424534")` → REST call → code saved to Redis → answer returned |
| 2 | "give me the trips" | Memory advisor injects turn 1 into the prompt → LLM routes to `getTrips`, typically passing `3424534` from history. **Even if it passes nothing**, the tool resolves the code from Redis state. |
| 2 (no code anywhere) | "give me the trips" (fresh conversation) | Tool throws → LLM asks the user for a national code |

## Production checklist

- **Persistence**: JDBC chat memory + Redis state with TTL — survives restarts and multiple app instances (both stores are shared, so the app stays stateless/horizontally scalable).
- **Security**: derive `userId` from the authenticated principal, never trust a client-sent conversation ID (prevents cross-user memory leakage). Consider prompt-injection hardening: validate the national code format in code (done above), not in the prompt.
- **Authorization**: before calling downstream APIs, verify this user is allowed to query that national code (add the check inside `resolveNationalCode` using `userId` from `ToolContext`).
- **Observability**: `SimpleLoggerAdvisor` + Micrometer; log every tool invocation with conversationId (mask the national code in logs — it's PII).
- **Resilience**: timeouts + retry + circuit breaker on every downstream call (done above).
- **Determinism**: low temperature + precise tool descriptions + a strict system prompt is what makes routing reliable.
- **Memory window tuning**: keep `maxMessages` big enough to cover realistic sessions; the Redis state store covers the case where the code scrolls out of the window.

This gives you LLM-driven routing with a deterministic safety net, so "give me the trips" always resolves to the right national code even in edge cases.
